cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "ResumeAI: calibrated resume and job-description matching with external validation"
message: >-
  If you refer to this study, please cite it. Read docs/DATA_CARD.md before reusing the
  data: the job descriptions are real and the match labels are synthetic, and every metric
  measures fidelity to a labelling rubric rather than to recruiter judgement.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Lepighe
    given-names: David
    orcid: ""
repository-code: "https://github.com/dlepighe1/Resume-jd-matcher"
url: "https://github.com/dlepighe1/Resume-jd-matcher"
abstract: >-
  A fine-tuned sentence-transformer that scores resume and job-description fit on a
  calibrated 0 to 1 scale, evaluated only on job postings it never trained on. The study
  reports a cross-encoder that scored 0.8917 Spearman on an internal test and -0.6122 on
  unseen postings, four anti-overfitting fixes that all failed to close that gap, a
  post-hoc calibration step that cuts absolute error by 29% without touching the ranking,
  and a pre-registered loss ablation. Differences between engines are reported with a
  paired cluster bootstrap over postings, Holm-corrected within families, and accompanied
  by equivalence tests and minimum detectable effects so that null results can be
  distinguished from underpowered ones.
keywords:
  - sentence-transformers
  - information retrieval
  - probability calibration
  - external validation
  - reproducibility
  - resume screening
license: MIT
version: "1.1.0"
date-released: "2026-08-05"
